1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:02,302
In traditional RAG,

2
00:00:02,302 --> 00:00:05,989
we first split the article into chunks of text.

3
00:00:05,989 --> 00:00:09,859
Then, we convert each chunk into a vector through embedding

4
00:00:09,926 --> 00:00:11,628
and store it in a vector database.

5
00:00:11,628 --> 00:00:13,430
For those who are not familiar with traditional RAG,

6
00:00:13,463 --> 00:00:15,231
you can refer to these two videos.

7
00:00:15,231 --> 00:00:17,534
They explain the principles and code implementation respectively,

8
00:00:17,534 --> 00:00:19,636
telling you everything about RAG.

9
00:00:19,636 --> 00:00:21,488
I will put the links in the video description.

10
00:00:21,488 --> 00:00:23,723
Here, let's take a simple example.

11
00:00:23,723 --> 00:00:25,492
If we chunk by sentence

12
00:00:25,492 --> 00:00:27,394
and store it in the vector database,

13
00:00:27,394 --> 00:00:29,963
then when a user asks what Wang likes to eat,

14
00:00:29,963 --> 00:00:31,498
the system can easily find

15
00:00:31,498 --> 00:00:32,866
"Wang likes to eat watermelon"

16
00:00:32,866 --> 00:00:34,834
and "Wang also likes to eat peaches"

17
00:00:34,834 --> 00:00:37,237
because their topics are highly relevant.

18
00:00:37,237 --> 00:00:39,289
But if you ask in this article

19
00:00:39,289 --> 00:00:41,091
how many times watermelon was mentioned,

20
00:00:41,091 --> 00:00:42,358
that would be a bit tricky.

21
00:00:42,525 --> 00:00:45,528
Although "watermelon" appears in many sentences,

22
00:00:45,528 --> 00:00:48,064
it is scattered across different fragments.

23
00:00:48,198 --> 00:00:51,167
No single sentence can answer this question alone.

24
00:00:51,167 --> 00:00:55,055
So RAG is likely to retrieve incorrectly or miss some information.

25
00:00:55,055 --> 00:00:56,389
Why does this happen?

26
00:00:56,389 --> 00:00:57,624
Let's look at the first sentence.

27
00:00:57,624 --> 00:00:59,159
"Wang likes to eat watermelon."

28
00:00:59,159 --> 00:01:01,428
This contains three layers of information:

29
00:01:01,428 --> 00:01:04,030
"Wang," "likes to eat," and "watermelon."

30
00:01:04,030 --> 00:01:05,432
In traditional RAG,

31
00:01:05,432 --> 00:01:08,134
we calculate relevance based on the entire passage.

32
00:01:08,134 --> 00:01:11,721
If the question is just "how many times is watermelon mentioned in the article,"

33
00:01:11,721 --> 00:01:14,824
then it has nothing to do with "Wang" and "likes to eat."

34
00:01:14,824 --> 00:01:17,393
Therefore, the overall relevance is not high enough.

35
00:01:17,393 --> 00:01:21,498
So when querying, it's possible to miss "Wang likes to eat watermelon."

36
00:01:21,498 --> 00:01:24,000
This is the first problem with traditional RAG.

37
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,887
The match between a global question and a local fragment is too low.

38
00:01:27,887 --> 00:01:29,289
So you might think,

39
00:01:29,289 --> 00:01:31,624
"Then I'll just cut the fragments even smaller,"

40
00:01:31,624 --> 00:01:33,393
for example, by word.

41
00:01:33,560 --> 00:01:35,995
This way, every "watermelon" will be a hit.

42
00:01:35,995 --> 00:01:39,199
This can certainly solve the problem of counting watermelons.

43
00:01:39,232 --> 00:01:40,533
But on the other hand,

44
00:01:40,633 --> 00:01:42,135
if we ask another question,

45
00:01:42,135 --> 00:01:44,154
"What does Wang like to eat?"

46
00:01:44,154 --> 00:01:46,456
RAG can't handle it anymore.

47
00:01:46,456 --> 00:01:49,425
Because the relationships between the information have been broken.

48
00:01:49,425 --> 00:01:50,894
So here comes the contradiction.

49
00:01:50,894 --> 00:01:53,229
If you cut it too large, you'll easily miss details.

50
00:01:53,229 --> 00:01:57,200
But if you cut it too small, you'll easily lose the semantic connections.

51
00:01:57,200 --> 00:02:01,087
This contradiction is one of the root causes of the inaccuracy of traditional RAG.

52
00:02:01,187 --> 00:02:03,890
So is there a way to not miss details

53
00:02:03,890 --> 00:02:06,192
and still retain the semantic structure?

54
00:02:06,192 --> 00:02:07,093
There actually is.

55
00:02:07,093 --> 00:02:10,013
That is the Knowledge Graph.

56
00:02:10,013 --> 00:02:14,367
GraphRAG uses a knowledge graph to solve the problems of traditional RAG.

57
00:02:14,367 --> 00:02:16,102
Let's take our previous example.

58
00:02:16,102 --> 00:02:17,654
"Wang loves to eat watermelon."

59
00:02:17,654 --> 00:02:20,423
If we convert it into the form of a knowledge graph,

60
00:02:20,423 --> 00:02:21,524
it looks like this.

61
00:02:21,524 --> 00:02:25,128
Here, "Wang" and "watermelon" are called Entities.

62
00:02:25,295 --> 00:02:28,765
The edge between them is a relationship, called a Relationship.

63
00:02:28,832 --> 00:02:30,733
These relationships are not just lines.

64
00:02:30,733 --> 00:02:31,968
You can also add attributes.

65
00:02:31,968 --> 00:02:33,870
For example, "loves to eat."

66
00:02:33,870 --> 00:02:34,954
Entities can also have attributes.

67
00:02:34,954 --> 00:02:36,756
For example, Wang is a person.

68
00:02:36,756 --> 00:02:38,391
Watermelon is a fruit.

69
00:02:38,391 --> 00:02:40,927
This graph structure that has both entities and relationships,

70
00:02:40,927 --> 00:02:44,597
and can also have attributes, is called an LPG,

71
00:02:44,597 --> 00:02:47,317
which stands for Labeled Property Graph.

72
00:02:47,317 --> 00:02:49,369
Here, let's first focus on how GraphRAG

73
00:02:49,369 --> 00:02:51,521
builds this knowledge graph.

74
00:02:51,521 --> 00:02:53,623
Then we'll worry about how to query it.

75
00:02:53,623 --> 00:02:55,725
Before large language models appeared,

76
00:02:55,758 --> 00:02:59,429
building a knowledge graph was a very, very complex process.

77
00:02:59,429 --> 00:03:00,463
The algorithms were obscure,

78
00:03:00,463 --> 00:03:01,698
and the results were not very good.

79
00:03:01,698 --> 00:03:03,566
We won't go into detail here.

80
00:03:03,566 --> 00:03:05,735
But now, the emergence of large models

81
00:03:05,735 --> 00:03:07,737
has greatly simplified this process.

82
00:03:07,787 --> 00:03:11,591
For example, the prompt for generating a knowledge graph in GraphRAG

83
00:03:11,591 --> 00:03:12,692
looks like this.

84
00:03:12,759 --> 00:03:16,429
It's a bit long. Let's use a simplified version to demonstrate the idea.

85
00:03:16,429 --> 00:03:19,399
Suppose we want to generate an LPG from the sentence "Wang loves to eat watermelon."

86
00:03:19,399 --> 00:03:20,800
generate an LPG.

87
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:23,052
We can write the prompt like this:

88
00:03:23,052 --> 00:03:26,189
"Goal: Generate an LPG for 'Wang likes to eat watermelon'."

89
00:03:26,222 --> 00:03:29,025
Then we give the core process.

90
00:03:29,058 --> 00:03:30,927
First is to identify entities,

91
00:03:31,194 --> 00:03:33,930
which are "Wang" and "watermelon" in this sentence.

92
00:03:33,930 --> 00:03:35,064
We can write it like this:

93
00:03:35,064 --> 00:03:35,899
Step 1:

94
00:03:35,899 --> 00:03:37,600
Identify entity types,

95
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:39,502
including person, object, and taste.

96
00:03:39,502 --> 00:04:01,624
Here, the entity types need to be manually set by us based on the text content.

97
00:03:44,190 --> 00:03:47,227
The default entity types used in GraphRAG

98
00:03:47,227 --> 00:03:50,230
include organization, person, location, and event.

99
00:03:50,330 --> 00:03:53,066
The second step is to identify the relationships between the entities.

100
00:03:53,166 --> 00:03:57,036
Both of these steps have formal names in technical terms.

101
00:03:57,036 --> 00:04:01,624
The first step is called Named Entity Recognition.

102
00:04:01,658 --> 00:04:05,061
The second step is called Relation Extraction.

103
00:04:05,061 --> 00:04:08,431
In the past, both of these tasks had very complex models and algorithms.

104
00:04:08,431 --> 00:04:11,734
Now, we can get it done with just one prompt.

105
00:04:11,734 --> 00:04:14,354
Then we define the output format.

106
00:04:14,420 --> 00:04:17,190
For example, entities and types are separated by a colon,

107
00:04:17,190 --> 00:04:20,026
and relationships between entities are indicated by arrows, and so on.

108
00:04:20,093 --> 00:04:23,429
In addition, GraphRAG will ask the large model to generate

109
00:04:23,863 --> 00:04:27,333
a brief text description for each entity and relationship.

110
00:04:27,333 --> 00:04:30,570
Then GraphRAG will take this large pile of prompts

111
00:04:30,570 --> 00:04:32,956
and send it all to the large language model.

112
00:04:32,956 --> 00:04:35,358
If the AI model is smart enough,

113
00:04:35,358 --> 00:04:38,861
the AI will return a format like this according to the requirements.

114
00:04:38,861 --> 00:04:42,332
It will identify Wang and watermelon as two entities,

115
00:04:42,332 --> 00:04:45,368
and a relationship that Wang loves to eat watermelon.

116
00:04:45,368 --> 00:04:48,488
Note that all this content is automatically generated by AI.

117
00:04:48,488 --> 00:04:50,590
It may not be exactly the same as the original text.

118
00:04:50,590 --> 00:04:52,792
The AI might even make up stories.

119
00:04:52,792 --> 00:04:56,262
So we usually have to add a warning to the prompt,

120
00:04:56,262 --> 00:04:58,331
telling the AI not to fabricate information.

121
00:04:58,364 --> 00:05:03,036
Just like that, GraphRAG parses the structured text returned by the AI.

122
00:05:03,036 --> 00:05:04,487
It's like a joke.

123
00:05:04,487 --> 00:05:05,555
"Wang loves to eat melon."

124
00:05:05,555 --> 00:05:08,624
The knowledge graph for this sentence is parsed out.

125
00:05:08,624 --> 00:05:10,760
Doesn't that sound a bit unreliable?

126
00:05:10,760 --> 00:05:12,095
But surprisingly,

127
00:05:12,095 --> 00:05:13,896
the effect is actually quite good.

128
00:05:13,963 --> 00:05:17,834
Because the knowledge graph itself is a very old concept.

129
00:05:17,834 --> 00:05:19,335
Long before large models,

130
00:05:19,335 --> 00:05:23,323
it has been frequently used in many public corpora.

131
00:05:23,323 --> 00:05:26,359
It turns out that large models are still very good at processing it.

132
00:05:26,359 --> 00:05:27,593
In GraphRAG,

133
00:05:27,593 --> 00:05:30,330
there is a particularly interesting little design here.

134
00:05:30,330 --> 00:05:33,299
Each time the AI finishes generating a knowledge graph,

135
00:05:33,299 --> 00:05:35,635
GraphRAG will take this knowledge graph

136
00:05:35,935 --> 00:05:38,488
and send it to the AI again along with the original text.

137
00:05:38,488 --> 00:05:40,990
Asking it, "Did you miss anything?"

138
00:05:40,990 --> 00:05:42,892
"Do you want to add anything else?"

139
00:05:42,892 --> 00:05:44,027
If the AI says yes,

140
00:05:44,027 --> 00:05:45,328
then it adds another round.

141
00:05:45,328 --> 00:05:46,996
Then ask again and add again.

142
00:05:46,996 --> 00:05:48,998
This process will loop continuously

143
00:05:48,998 --> 00:05:50,933
until the AI admits it itself,

144
00:05:50,933 --> 00:05:53,636
"I have nothing more to add."

145
00:05:53,636 --> 00:05:55,922
Now the programmers have finally found

146
00:05:55,922 --> 00:05:57,690
an object they can bully too.

147
00:05:57,890 --> 00:06:00,793
This process of repeated questioning and extracting information

148
00:06:00,793 --> 00:06:03,730
has a specific term called Data Gleaning.

149
00:06:03,730 --> 00:06:06,232
I haven't found a Chinese translation for it yet.

150
00:06:06,232 --> 00:06:08,835
Let's just call it "large model PUA" for now.

151
00:06:08,835 --> 00:06:12,655
So GraphRAG will for each paragraph of text in the article

152
00:06:12,789 --> 00:06:15,758
generate such a knowledge graph separately.

153
00:06:15,758 --> 00:06:18,528
We've already talked about "Wang likes to eat melons."

154
00:06:18,528 --> 00:06:20,363
The next two sentences are actually quite similar.

155
00:06:20,363 --> 00:06:21,898
Let's pay attention here.

156
00:06:21,998 --> 00:06:23,132
The last sentence.

157
00:06:23,132 --> 00:06:26,402
The taste "sweet" in it is also identified as an entity.

158
00:06:26,402 --> 00:06:29,188
This is because "taste" in our prompt

159
00:06:29,188 --> 00:06:30,656
is defined as an entity.

160
00:06:30,656 --> 00:06:33,159
After all the fragments have been processed,

161
00:06:33,159 --> 00:06:37,497
GraphRAG will automatically merge entities with the same name.

162
00:06:37,597 --> 00:06:39,599
For example, merging all the "Wang"s,

163
00:06:39,599 --> 00:06:41,968
merging all the "watermelon"s, and so on.

164
00:06:41,968 --> 00:06:46,155
Finally, it will piece together a complete article-level knowledge graph.

165
00:06:46,155 --> 00:06:48,491
This process does not require AI participation.

166
00:06:48,491 --> 00:06:50,359
It is done automatically by the program.

167
00:06:50,359 --> 00:06:52,028
But during the merging process,

168
00:06:52,028 --> 00:06:55,965
the descriptive information originally scattered in different nodes or edges

169
00:06:56,032 --> 00:06:58,234
will also be integrated together.

170
00:06:58,234 --> 00:07:00,002
Our example is relatively simple.

171
00:07:00,002 --> 00:07:02,855
Only the descriptive information of the nodes has been merged.

172
00:07:02,855 --> 00:07:04,657
But in actual use,

173
00:07:04,657 --> 00:07:07,627
the description of the edge will also be included in the merge.

174
00:07:07,627 --> 00:07:12,131
Then, at this time, GraphRAG will take each merged descriptive information

175
00:07:12,131 --> 00:07:13,666
and give it to the large language model.

176
00:07:13,733 --> 00:07:17,386
Please generate a more complete based on these descriptions,

177
00:07:17,386 --> 00:07:19,889
a more fluent summary description.

178
00:07:19,889 --> 00:07:23,526
And use it as the final description after merging.

179
00:07:23,526 --> 00:07:28,131
This way, what we finally get is not only a structurally clear graph,

180
00:07:28,131 --> 00:07:32,368
but also a natural language description organized by AI.

181
00:07:32,368 --> 00:07:34,754
There is a detail worth mentioning.

182
00:07:34,754 --> 00:07:37,123
GraphRAG has been maintaining the

183
00:07:37,123 --> 00:07:40,259
correspondence between the knowledge graph and the original text.

184
00:07:40,259 --> 00:07:42,562
For example, the entity "Wang" in the graph,

185
00:07:42,562 --> 00:07:47,200
GraphRAG knows which paragraphs of the original text this information was generated from.

186
00:07:47,200 --> 00:07:48,334
The reverse is also true.

187
00:07:48,468 --> 00:07:49,635
Give it a paragraph of original text,

188
00:07:49,635 --> 00:07:50,736
it can also tell you

189
00:07:50,736 --> 00:07:55,191
which entities and relationships this sentence corresponds to in the knowledge graph.

190
00:07:55,191 --> 00:07:57,994
This will be used in the query later.

191
00:07:57,994 --> 00:08:01,764
Now we have the knowledge graph corresponding to the article.

192
00:08:01,931 --> 00:08:03,733
But if the article is very long,

193
00:08:03,733 --> 00:08:07,036
this graph will become very large and complex.

194
00:08:07,036 --> 00:08:09,355
Querying it is actually not that convenient.

195
00:08:09,355 --> 00:08:12,425
So GraphRAG did one more step of processing.

196
00:08:12,425 --> 00:08:15,761
It will use a method called Leiden community detection

197
00:08:15,761 --> 00:08:19,665
to merge nodes with dense edges in the graph into a whole.

198
00:08:19,665 --> 00:08:21,868
Please study the specific algorithm yourself.

199
00:08:21,868 --> 00:08:23,436
Here we only look at the results.

200
00:08:23,436 --> 00:08:25,721
But our graph is really too simple,

201
00:08:25,721 --> 00:08:27,723
so here we can only give a rough idea.

202
00:08:27,723 --> 00:08:31,627
If the four nodes on the left have many edges between them,

203
00:08:31,694 --> 00:08:33,462
they can be combined into a whole.

204
00:08:33,462 --> 00:08:36,632
And the "taste" on the right can be a separate whole.

205
00:08:36,666 --> 00:08:40,102
Then, just like merging entities with the same name before,

206
00:08:40,203 --> 00:08:43,489
GraphRAG will let the AI use the information from each subgraph's

207
00:08:43,489 --> 00:08:45,324
node and edge description information

208
00:08:45,324 --> 00:08:48,427
to generate a more advanced summary description.

209
00:08:48,427 --> 00:08:50,730
For example, if we take the descriptions of all the nodes and edges

210
00:08:50,730 --> 00:08:53,533
in the left subgraph,

211
00:08:53,533 --> 00:08:56,736
AI can summarize that Wang loves to eat melons and peaches.

212
00:08:56,736 --> 00:08:58,187
Xiao Wang loves to eat melons.

213
00:08:58,187 --> 00:08:59,855
And this summarizing process

214
00:08:59,855 --> 00:09:02,425
is not just a simple abstraction of information.

215
00:09:02,425 --> 00:09:05,428
It can often infer additional information.

216
00:09:05,428 --> 00:09:07,697
For example, Xiao Wang only likes to eat melons.

217
00:09:07,830 --> 00:09:10,333
Combined with Wang's love for melons and peaches,

218
00:09:10,333 --> 00:09:13,436
it can be inferred that Xiao Wang does not like to eat peaches.

219
00:09:13,502 --> 00:09:16,522
This sentence never appeared in the original text.

220
00:09:16,522 --> 00:09:19,992
This is where the knowledge graph surpasses simple text matching

221
00:09:19,992 --> 00:09:22,562
and truly begins to understand and think.

222
00:09:22,562 --> 00:09:26,132
This way, we get a simplified knowledge graph.

223
00:09:26,132 --> 00:09:30,069
Each node represents a local information block in the original graph.

224
00:09:30,069 --> 00:09:32,154
If this graph is not simple enough,

225
00:09:32,154 --> 00:09:34,290
then continue to repeat this process.

226
00:09:34,290 --> 00:09:35,891
Merge again, summarize again.

227
00:09:35,891 --> 00:09:37,093
Abstract further up.

228
00:09:37,093 --> 00:09:38,527
Layer by layer upwards,

229
00:09:38,527 --> 00:09:42,031
eventually forming a hierarchical structure of a knowledge graph.

230
00:09:42,031 --> 00:09:43,566
The higher up in this structure,

231
00:09:43,566 --> 00:09:45,935
the more abstract and refined the information becomes.

232
00:09:46,102 --> 00:09:49,689
The further down, the closer to the original text and the more specific the details.

233
00:09:49,689 --> 00:09:52,692
Alright, now we have this knowledge-rich,

234
00:09:52,692 --> 00:09:55,061
well-structured knowledge graph.

235
00:09:55,061 --> 00:09:57,363
The final step is to turn it into a

236
00:09:57,430 --> 00:10:00,433
quickly searchable index library.

237
00:10:00,433 --> 00:10:03,502
This brings us back to our familiar operation, embedding.

238
00:10:03,502 --> 00:10:06,255
We take each node in the knowledge graph,

239
00:10:06,255 --> 00:10:09,692
the entity information and summary description of each edge,

240
00:10:09,692 --> 00:10:13,062
and treat them all as a text fragment for embedding.

241
00:10:13,062 --> 00:10:15,331
Then store them in a vector database.

242
00:10:15,331 --> 00:10:18,701
In addition, each slice from the original text at the beginning

243
00:10:18,701 --> 00:10:20,803
is also stored in the vector database.

244
00:10:20,820 --> 00:10:23,789
This is the final form of the knowledge graph.

245
00:10:23,789 --> 00:10:26,926
Actually, the subsequent query becomes very simple.

246
00:10:26,926 --> 00:10:28,561
Just like a regular RAG,

247
00:10:28,628 --> 00:10:30,363
we take the user's question,

248
00:10:30,363 --> 00:10:31,931
for example, "What does Wang like to eat?"

249
00:10:31,931 --> 00:10:34,233
do an embedding to also convert it into a vector.

250
00:10:34,300 --> 00:10:36,669
Then we can use it to match against the original text

251
00:10:36,669 --> 00:10:38,688
or the embedding of the knowledge graph.

252
00:10:38,688 --> 00:10:39,655
to perform matching.

253
00:10:39,655 --> 00:10:41,691
Matching is very flexible.

254
00:10:41,691 --> 00:10:44,660
We can query only a certain layer of the knowledge graph,

255
00:10:44,660 --> 00:10:45,761
or a few layers,

256
00:10:45,761 --> 00:10:47,663
or we can query only the original text.

257
00:10:47,763 --> 00:10:50,132
We can even query everything.

258
00:10:50,132 --> 00:10:52,335
There are no fixed rules for this process.

259
00:10:52,335 --> 00:10:54,170
For example, in GraphRAG,

260
00:10:54,170 --> 00:10:57,023
it provides a strategy called "local search."

261
00:10:57,023 --> 00:11:00,092
It will first start from the bottom layer of the knowledge graph

262
00:11:00,092 --> 00:11:02,895
to find the entity that is closest to the question.

263
00:11:02,895 --> 00:11:04,497
Remember we mentioned earlier

264
00:11:04,497 --> 00:11:09,101
that GraphRAG maintains the mapping relationship between the graph and the original text?

265
00:11:09,101 --> 00:11:11,620
Once a relevant graph point is found,

266
00:11:11,854 --> 00:11:14,323
GraphRAG can reverse-lookup

267
00:11:14,323 --> 00:11:17,593
which original text these points and edges were generated from.

268
00:11:17,593 --> 00:11:18,994
And these points and edges,

269
00:11:18,994 --> 00:11:22,198
which upper-level graph structures do they appear in?

270
00:11:22,198 --> 00:11:25,301
What are their neighboring points and edges?

271
00:11:25,334 --> 00:11:27,737
As for the "Covariate" at the bottom,

272
00:11:27,737 --> 00:11:30,990
it's equivalent to a textual summary of the original text snippet.

273
00:11:30,990 --> 00:11:32,558
It's an experimental feature.

274
00:11:32,558 --> 00:11:34,560
I personally think it's a bit redundant.

275
00:11:34,560 --> 00:11:36,495
Friends who are interested can read

276
00:11:36,662 --> 00:11:41,167
the extract_covariates and claim_extractor files.

277
00:11:41,167 --> 00:11:44,387
Then GraphRAG will take all the found

278
00:11:44,387 --> 00:11:46,956
summary descriptions of these points and edges,

279
00:11:46,956 --> 00:11:49,458
and their associated original text snippets,

280
00:11:49,458 --> 00:11:51,560
plus the user's question,

281
00:11:51,560 --> 00:11:52,995
"What does Wang like to eat?"

282
00:11:52,995 --> 00:11:55,664
and package them all together to send to the AI large model.

283
00:11:55,664 --> 00:11:59,502
This way, whether you're asking a high-level abstract question

284
00:11:59,502 --> 00:12:02,154
or a detailed question about a specific passage of the original text,

285
00:12:02,154 --> 00:12:04,957
the relevant information will be brought along.

286
00:12:05,024 --> 00:12:09,228
Because local search starts from the bottom level of the graph,

287
00:12:09,261 --> 00:12:12,098
so it's particularly good at handling detail-rich,

288
00:12:12,098 --> 00:12:13,632
precisely located questions.

289
00:12:13,632 --> 00:12:16,669
This is also why it's called local search.

290
00:12:16,669 --> 00:12:20,523
GraphRAG also has another query strategy called global search.

291
00:12:20,589 --> 00:12:22,124
Contrary to local search,

292
00:12:22,124 --> 00:12:25,594
global search starts from the higher levels of the graph.

293
00:12:25,594 --> 00:12:28,097
and then traces down layer by layer.

294
00:12:28,097 --> 00:12:29,365
So its advantage is that

295
00:12:29,365 --> 00:12:32,902
it is more suitable than local search for answering questions that are a bit more abstract

296
00:12:32,902 --> 00:12:34,954
and have a stronger global nature.

297
00:12:34,954 --> 00:12:37,990
For example, "What is the core idea of this article?"

298
00:12:37,990 --> 00:12:40,926
In short, this is the overall architecture of GraphRAG.

299
00:12:41,160 --> 00:12:44,230
From building the knowledge graph, to summarizing descriptions,

300
00:12:44,230 --> 00:12:47,833
almost every step is inseparable from the participation of large language models.

301
00:12:47,833 --> 00:12:50,102
It does sound a bit unreliable.

302
00:12:50,102 --> 00:12:53,122
But according to the experimental data in Microsoft's paper,

303
00:12:53,122 --> 00:12:55,024
the results are actually quite good.

304
00:12:55,024 --> 00:12:56,525
So it's not hard to understand

305
00:12:56,525 --> 00:13:00,196
why everyone is complaining that GraphRAG is too token-intensive.

306
00:13:00,196 --> 00:13:02,097
Because it is essentially a kind of

307
00:13:02,097 --> 00:13:05,768
design that allows large language models to permeate the entire process.

308
00:13:05,768 --> 00:13:07,837
You can say it's extravagant and burns money,

309
00:13:07,837 --> 00:13:09,655
but you can't say it's not serious.

310
00:13:13,058 --> 00:13:17,096
The process of GraphRAG is actually very similar to our learning journey.

311
00:13:17,096 --> 00:13:22,334
Initially, our minds are filled with scattered knowledge points and isolated notes,

312
00:13:22,334 --> 00:13:25,621
just like those unorganized text fragments.

313
00:13:25,621 --> 00:13:28,157
With continuous accumulation and thinking,

314
00:13:28,157 --> 00:13:30,726
those points slowly form lines,

315
00:13:30,759 --> 00:13:33,462
and eventually weave a web in our minds,

316
00:13:33,596 --> 00:13:35,798
our own knowledge graph.

317
00:13:35,798 --> 00:13:38,534
And our perspective in this process

318
00:13:38,567 --> 00:13:40,402
is raised bit by bit.

319
00:13:40,402 --> 00:13:43,022
But I think the most wonderful part is

320
00:13:43,022 --> 00:13:44,356
that in this process,

321
00:13:44,356 --> 00:13:46,692
we can even discover some

322
00:13:46,692 --> 00:13:48,828
connections that didn't exist before.

323
00:13:49,195 --> 00:13:52,431
Just like me sharing GraphRAG now,

324
00:13:52,431 --> 00:13:55,167
and you quietly listening in front of the screen.

325
00:13:55,167 --> 00:13:57,069
Although we have never met,

326
00:13:57,069 --> 00:13:59,622
but because of a sense of curiosity,

327
00:13:59,822 --> 00:14:02,124
a tacit understanding arises in our hearts.

328
00:14:02,124 --> 00:14:05,995
Perhaps this is the romance that belongs to knowledge seekers.

